1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,279
Language models predict the next word. A reasoner derives the next step.

2
00:00:05,559 --> 00:00:09,996
In five minutes: what symbolic reasoning is, what a theorem prover does,

3
00:00:09,996 --> 00:00:14,371
where an LLM fits around one, and a small proof in nibli, step by step.

4
00:00:15,121 --> 00:00:19,579
Symbolic reasoning works on explicit statements and explicit rules.

5
00:00:19,859 --> 00:00:25,142
Here are three: every dog is an animal, every animal eats, and Adam is a dog.

6
00:00:25,422 --> 00:00:28,765
Nothing is learned from examples, and nothing is guessed.

7
00:00:29,045 --> 00:00:33,887
A conclusion counts only if it follows from these statements by fixed rules of inference.

8
00:00:34,167 --> 00:00:36,791
Run it again tomorrow and you get the same answer,

9
00:00:36,791 --> 00:00:39,310
and you can inspect every step that produced it.

10
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:42,301
A theorem prover automates that.

11
00:00:42,581 --> 00:00:46,156
You give it premises and a claim, and it searches for a proof:

12
00:00:46,156 --> 00:00:49,616
a chain of rule applications from the premises to the claim.

13
00:00:49,896 --> 00:00:54,076
If it finds one, the answer is TRUE, and it can show you the chain.

14
00:00:54,356 --> 00:00:57,351
That answer is sound with respect to what you encoded.

15
00:00:57,631 --> 00:01:01,857
It doesn't make your premises true. It tells you what they commit you to.

16
00:01:02,137 --> 00:01:08,387
nibli answers with one of four verdicts: TRUE; FALSE, meaning not derivable; UNKNOWN,

17
00:01:08,387 --> 00:01:14,490
with a reason; and RESOURCE_EXCEEDED, when a depth, fuel or memory budget runs out.

18
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:19,025
A neuro-symbolic system pairs a neural front end with a symbolic core,

19
00:01:19,025 --> 00:01:20,917
and lets each do what it's good at.

20
00:01:21,197 --> 00:01:23,752
Language models are good at reading prose.

21
00:01:24,032 --> 00:01:30,719
So in nibli, an LLM can formalize plain English into nibli KR, the engine's rule language.

22
00:01:30,999 --> 00:01:34,076
That draft sits outside the reasoning firewall.

23
00:01:34,356 --> 00:01:38,373
Before anything reasons over it, deterministic gates check it.

24
00:01:38,653 --> 00:01:44,826
The grammar. A committed vocabulary, where an unknown word is an error instead of a guess.

25
00:01:44,826 --> 00:01:49,148
The semantic compiler. And a round trip back to canonical text.

26
00:01:49,428 --> 00:01:53,014
Then a person reviews the rules and their back-translation:

27
00:01:53,014 --> 00:01:56,174
a fixed, mechanical reading, with no model involved.

28
00:01:56,454 --> 00:01:58,298
This example comes pre-encoded,

29
00:01:58,298 --> 00:02:02,166
so the Formalize button is switched off and we skip the drafting.

30
00:02:02,446 --> 00:02:05,963
The LLM drafts. The engine decides.

31
00:02:06,713 --> 00:02:09,349
nibli is the engine I build in Rust.

32
00:02:09,629 --> 00:02:12,694
It's a theorem prover, and it's open source.

33
00:02:12,974 --> 00:02:15,929
It compiles nibli KR into first-order logic,

34
00:02:15,929 --> 00:02:19,824
and answers claims by backward chaining over a fact store.

35
00:02:20,104 --> 00:02:23,117
The whole pipeline, parser, compiler and reasoner,

36
00:02:23,117 --> 00:02:28,660
runs right here in the browser as WebAssembly, and also under Wasmtime or as a native crate.

37
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:32,203
It never invents a fact, a rule or a proof step.

38
00:02:32,953 --> 00:02:36,900
Here's the syllogism example: three lines of nibli KR.

39
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:42,498
The first reads: for every X, if X is a dog, then X is an animal.

40
00:02:42,778 --> 00:02:47,561
The second: for every X, if X is an animal, then X eats.

41
00:02:47,841 --> 00:02:50,012
The third: Adam is a dog.

42
00:02:50,292 --> 00:02:55,006
We don't ask a question. We state the claim to check: Adam eats.

43
00:02:55,756 --> 00:02:58,879
The engine starts from the claim and works backward.

44
00:02:59,159 --> 00:03:03,234
The goal is: Adam eats. No stored fact says so.

45
00:03:03,514 --> 00:03:08,878
So it looks for a rule whose conclusion is eats: line two, every animal eats.

46
00:03:09,158 --> 00:03:12,424
Matching that conclusion to the goal binds X to Adam,

47
00:03:12,424 --> 00:03:14,951
and leaves a new goal: Adam is an animal.

48
00:03:15,231 --> 00:03:19,810
Again there's no stored fact, so it looks for a rule that concludes animal:

49
00:03:19,810 --> 00:03:21,825
line one, every dog is an animal.

50
00:03:22,105 --> 00:03:25,972
That leaves one goal, Adam is a dog, and that one is stored.

51
00:03:26,252 --> 00:03:28,852
The chain closes in two rule steps.

52
00:03:29,132 --> 00:03:33,753
To find the shortest chain, the engine first runs a cheap search without recording,

53
00:03:33,753 --> 00:03:37,538
raising the depth limit one step at a time until the claim resolves.

54
00:03:38,288 --> 00:03:41,145
Then it runs the search once more at that depth,

55
00:03:41,145 --> 00:03:44,894
this time recording every step. That record is the proof trace.

56
00:03:45,174 --> 00:03:48,076
The stored fact becomes a leaf, marked given.

57
00:03:48,356 --> 00:03:52,223
Each rule step becomes a node that cites the source line it used.

58
00:03:52,503 --> 00:03:56,938
“Adam eats” cites recorded source #1: every animal eats.

59
00:03:57,218 --> 00:04:02,233
“Adam is an animal” cites recorded source #0: every dog is an animal.

60
00:04:02,513 --> 00:04:06,391
Open the role-level detail, and you see what those lines are made of.

61
00:04:06,671 --> 00:04:09,899
The fact is stored as an event with named roles:

62
00:04:09,899 --> 00:04:12,859
the dog, Adam, and a breed nobody specified.

63
00:04:13,139 --> 00:04:15,478
When the same sub-goal comes up again,

64
00:04:15,478 --> 00:04:20,093
the trace says “proved above” and points back, instead of proving it twice.

65
00:04:20,373 --> 00:04:24,263
The playground folds the whole record into the three lines you saw,

66
00:04:24,263 --> 00:04:25,714
with a plain-English why.

67
00:04:26,464 --> 00:04:28,681
Now the claim that Adam is a bird.

68
00:04:28,961 --> 00:04:32,793
Nothing in the knowledge base supports it, so the answer is FALSE.

69
00:04:33,073 --> 00:04:37,194
The why line says no example could be found that satisfies the claim.

70
00:04:37,474 --> 00:04:40,962
Here, FALSE means not derivable from what we asserted.

71
00:04:40,962 --> 00:04:43,546
It isn't a proof that Adam isn't a bird.

72
00:04:43,826 --> 00:04:46,810
nibli won't claim more than its premises support.

73
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:53,295
Try it at dhilipsiva.dev/nibli-playground. The code is on GitHub.

74
00:04:53,575 --> 00:04:56,486
It's also the running example in my book,

75
00:04:56,486 --> 00:05:02,167
Ultimate Neuro-Symbolic Reasoning for AI Engineers, scheduled for December 2026.

76
00:05:02,447 --> 00:05:03,886
I'm dhilipsiva.

